{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,28]],"date-time":"2026-10-28T18:32:48Z","timestamp":1793212368966,"version":"4.5.0"},"reference-count":93,"publisher":"National Academy of Sciences","issue":"44","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,28]],"date-time":"2026-10-28T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1793145600000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0\/"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100002842","name":"Chiang Mai University","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["N\/A"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["N\/A"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100002842","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"name":"The Dental Association of Thailand","award":["N\/A"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["N\/A"]}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100010724","name":"Health Systems Research Institute","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["N\/A"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["N\/A"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100010724","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100003422","name":"KAUST | Global Collaborative Research, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["N\/A"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["N\/A"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100003422","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"name":"National Institute of Health","award":["R35 DE027557"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["R35 DE027557"]}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100000268","name":"UKRI | Biotechnology and Biological Sciences Research Council","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["BB\/W00240X\/1"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["BB\/W00240X\/1"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100000268","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"name":"KAUST Center of Excellence for Smart Health","award":["5932"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["5932"]}],"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/02y9qjz38","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"crossref"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":["www.pnas.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,3]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    Primary cilia play an essential role in molecular signaling transduction during development, coordinating cell fate decisions and morphogenesis. Defects in components of the primary cilium result in an array of developmental syndromes called ciliopathies. Here we report 24 patients with nine rare and seven novel variants in the primary cilia gene\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">C2CD3,<\/jats:italic>\n                    which has previously been implicated in the ciliopathy Orofaciodigital syndrome XIV. Patients displayed an array of dental defects impacting the number, shape, and timing of tooth development. To understand the etiology underlying these defects, murine and avian models were analyzed during embryonic development.\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">\n                      Ap2Cre;C2cd3\n                      <jats:sup>ex4-5 fl\/fl<\/jats:sup>\n                    <\/jats:italic>\n                    mouse mutants showed a range of dental defects prenatally, mimicking several of the phenotypes observed in patients.\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">Shh<\/jats:italic>\n                    was upregulated in the developing upper jaw, leading to a widening of the midline, while\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">Shh<\/jats:italic>\n                    expression in the lower jaw was reduced, highlighting the context-dependent impact of cilia on hedgehog signaling. In avian mutants carrying a\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">C2CD3<\/jats:italic>\n                    variant,\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">\n                      talpid\n                      <jats:sup>2<\/jats:sup>\n                    <\/jats:italic>\n                    , tooth buds were evident with\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">SHH<\/jats:italic>\n                    expression upregulated during early development in both upper and lower jaws. Cartilage defects were additionally observed in both conditional mouse knockouts and\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">\n                      talpid\n                      <jats:sup>2<\/jats:sup>\n                    <\/jats:italic>\n                    embryos. These changes in Shh signaling provide a mechanism to explain the phenotypes in patients and highlight the conserved role of C2CD3 in coordinating the morphogenetic potential of the jaw and patterning of the forming dentition. Given the conserved phenotypes, alterations in C2CD3-dependent ciliogenesis may have played a role in modulating Shh signaling to alter the timing and position of tooth germs across vertebrate evolution.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1073\/pnas.2603057123","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,28]],"date-time":"2026-10-28T17:37:24Z","timestamp":1793209044000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1073\/pnas.cm10313","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["C2CD3 links tooth patterning to evolutionary tooth loss in birds and dental anomalies across vertebrates"],"prefix":"10.1073","volume":"123","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9841-0881","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Piranit","family":"Kantaputra","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/05m2fqn25","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Division of Pediatric Dentistry, Department of Orthodontics and Pediatric Dentistry, Faculty of Dentistry, Chiang Mai University","place":["Chiang Mai, Thailand"]},{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/05m2fqn25","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Division of Pediatric Dentistry, Center of Excellence in Medical Genetics Research, Faculty of Dentistry, Chiang Mai University","place":["Chiang